"""Build the curated manuscript directly from current exports and selection decisions."""
from pathlib import Path
import csv,html,subprocess,json,hashlib
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];O=R/'publications';read=lambda p:list(csv.DictReader(p.open()));rows=read(R/'catalog.csv');panels=read(R/'panels.csv');taxa=list(dict.fromkeys(x['target_taxon'] for x in panels));esc=html.escape
TITLE='MiPhyllo: taxon-stratified 16S and 28S PCR primer candidates for environmental DNA metabarcoding of Phyllodocida'
parts=[]
def page(s):parts.append('<section class="page">'+s+'</section>')
def table(headers,body):return '<table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+esc(x)+'</th>' for x in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+esc(str(x))+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>' for row in body)+'</tbody></table>'
page(f'''<h1>{TITLE}</h1><p class="author">Fei Xia</p><p class="author">Computational preprint · Curated edition · 5 November 2026</p><h2>Abstract</h2><p>Broad taxonomic scope can conflict with reference coverage and background specificity when designing environmental DNA primers for marine annelids. We curated mitochondrial 16S and nuclear 28S candidates from searches of eight nested Phyllodocida target units. Reference panels retained one sequence per named species. Ordinary and degenerate (D) primers shared binding endpoints, with degeneracy limited to eight variants per oligo. The initial collection contained 94 standard pairs and eight relaxed-background alternatives. We retained standard ordinary/D sets when D coverage reached at least 50% and at least one reference species matched exactly, while withdrawing order-wide Phyllodocida and suborder-wide Nereidiformia designs. These operational catalogue criteria retained 24 sets, comprising 48 primer pairs and 96 oligos, across six units. Nereididae 16S LD covered 58/61 references with 11 exact matches; Hesionidae 28S SD covered 40/45 with eight exact matches. Coverage and exact matching remained markedly different for several retained designs. All retained sequences and scores are unchanged subsets of the 102 originally independently re-scanned pairs. The other 54 pairs, source results and previous preprint are archived. The curated collection supports target-specific candidate selection; the thresholds and matching results do not constitute experimental validation.</p><h2>1. Introduction</h2><p>Phyllodocida comprises divergent polychaete lineages for which a single short rRNA assay may not reconcile coverage and specificity. Scale-worm phylogenetic reference data provide an established starting point [1], while environmental DNA has been applied to marine metazoan surveys [2]. MiFish illustrates the design principle of conserved primer-binding regions surrounding variable sequence [3]. Here that principle guides annelid candidate searches without transferring fish-primer sequences or assuming equivalent performance.</p><p>The original searches considered the order, four suborders and three families. The present revision separates useful candidate sets from low-coverage exploratory outcomes. Phyllodocida remains the scope of MiPhyllo, but its order-wide assay is no longer in the current catalogue. Nereidiformia is represented by three family-level panels. Retained and removed designs are traceable to the same frozen source data.</p>''')
page('''<h2>2. Materials and methods</h2><h3>2.1 References and design</h3><p>Each gene panel retained one reference per named species, prioritizing sequence completeness during source curation. The original units were Phyllodocida; Aphroditiformia, Glyceriformia, Nereidiformia and Phyllodociformia; and Syllidae, Nereididae and Hesionidae. Nested panels are not independent samples. Direction-adjusted MAFFT alignments [4] guided homologous windows; accession metadata, alignments and frozen backgrounds are distributed with each unit.</p><p>Selected spans use S 100-200 bp, M 201-300 bp and L 301-480 bp; original narrower search ranges remain in source scripts. Expanded searches used 18-30 nt oligos and 1 nt start increments, with frequent haplotypes, consensus seeds, staged coverage/exact-match ranking and local refinement. Ordinary/D pairs share all four binding endpoints. Degeneracy is ≤8 per oligo and limited to its 3′ half; the ordinary sequence need not be contained in its D expansion. Thermodynamic filters used 50 nM per variant and 200 nM per direction, minimum predicted Tm ≥45°C, F/R midpoint difference ≤5°C and structure Tm &lt;55°C.</p><h3>2.2 Matching and background selection</h3><p>Target coverage required ≤2 mismatches per end, compatible terminal three bases, total product length 100-500 bp and internal length ≥50 bp. Exact matching required zero mismatches at both ends, with IUPAC compatibility treated as a match. All reference species, including fragments lacking the complete window, remain in the denominator. Background scans permit internal gaps of 0-1000 bp. Strict WC/GT allows ≤2 mismatches and requires terminal compatibility; loose WC allows ≤3 mismatches and loose GT ≤2 without terminal constraints. GT additionally permits primer-oriented G:A and T:C compatibility as a sensitivity model.</p><p>The original Aphroditiformia study used a six-group polychaete background and limits of ten strict and fifty loose hits per model. Later suborder/family searches used expanded marine and non-target Phyllodocida references, strict WC≤1%, GT≤3%, loose WC≤5%, GT≤10%, and each strict close-relative count≤max(2, floor(3% × close-relative N)). Marine screening used inherited genus-level WoRMS assignments. Background composition differs between panels.</p><h3>2.3 Catalogue curation and verification</h3><p>We retained standard ordinary/D sets with D reference coverage ≥50% and at least one exact reference match, preserving the original full background profile. Phyllodocida and Nereidiformia broad-target designs were withdrawn. All alternatives selected without a loose-model veto were archived. The thresholds are operational editorial criteria, not empirically established efficacy thresholds. Ordinary counterparts remain coordinate-matched comparators and need not themselves reach 50% coverage. Lengths are not removed merely because another length has greater coverage.</p><p>The retained 48 pairs are sequence- and score-identical subsets of the 102 originally scanned pairs, for which C++ and independent Python implementations agreed record by record. This revision checks selection membership, unchanged sequences and statistics, FASTA exports, endpoint identity, degeneracy, bilingual tables and archive completeness. It introduces no fresh sequence design, experimental validation, common-panel evaluation or independent holdout test.</p>''')
body=[]
for t in taxa:
 ps={x['gene']:x for x in panels if x['target_taxon']==t};bands=lambda g:'/'.join(x['band'] for x in rows if x['target_taxon']==t and x['gene']==g and x['variant']=='D') or 'None'
 body.append([t,ps['16S']['target_species'],bands('16S'),ps['28S']['target_species'],bands('28S')])
page('<h2>3. Results</h2><h3>3.1 Retained units and length classes</h3>'+table(['Target unit','16S N','16S lengths','28S N','28S lengths'],body)+'''<p><b>Table 1.</b> Available reference species and retained lengths. Each retained length includes an ordinary and a D pair. Syllidae retains its 28S reference library but no current 28S primer. Counts describe each frozen panel and must not be summed across nested units.</p><img src="../figures/catalog_coverage.png"><p><b>Figure 1.</b> D coverage (filled) and exact matching (open) in the retained catalogue. Connectors show their difference. Missing lengths were archived or had no original candidate. Percentages use each panel's own denominator and are not common-panel performance comparisons.</p><p>Six target units retain 24 ordinary/D sets (48 pairs, 96 oligos). Neither the order-wide Phyllodocida nor suborder-wide Nereidiformia candidates remain. Phyllodociformia references represent Phyllodocidae only. Glyceriformia references do not include Lacydoniidae. Appendix A lists every retained sequence and its matching outcomes.</p>''')
page('''<h2>3.2 Selection tradeoffs and archived outcomes</h2><p>The 94 standard and eight conditional original pairs partition into 48 retained and 54 archived pairs. Phyllodocida designs covered at most 71/515 16S and 53/302 28S references; Nereidiformia reached at most 62/315 and 45/133. These outcomes remain available as exploratory results rather than recommended broad-target assays.</p><p>Further removals include Aphroditiformia 16S M/L, Glyceriformia 16S L, Syllidae 16S M/L and all its 28S candidates, and Nereididae 28S M/L. Phyllodociformia 16S M and 28S L were archived because their D candidates had no exact reference matches despite coverage above 50%. All eight conditional pairs were archived: relaxed Syllidae alternatives had substantial loose-background matching, whereas the Nereididae 28S alternative also remained below 50% coverage. Per-set decisions and original scores are distributed in the archive.</p><p>Retained designs still show tradeoffs. Aphroditiformia 16S SD covers 62/94 but matches only one species exactly; Glyceriformia 16S SD/MD each cover 14/20 with one exact match. Syllidae 16S SD covers 110/180 with seven exact matches. Nereididae 16S LD covers 58/61 with 11 exact matches but has strict WC/GT background counts of 30/58, compared with 0/12 for SD (57/61 covered). Nereididae 28S SD covers 29/52 with eight exact matches and zero hits in all four background models. Hesionidae 28S SD covers 40/45 with eight exact matches.</p><h2>4. Discussion</h2><p>Catalogue inclusion should reflect a plausible target scope and transparent tradeoffs, not the mere existence of a search result. The present pruning removes clearly weak broad-target outcomes and avoids filling every gene-by-length cell. It preserves different retained lengths because coverage alone does not establish their equivalence for fragmented DNA or the taxonomic resolution of the internal amplicon.</p><p>The 50% coverage and nonzero-exact criteria provide a reproducible editorial boundary. They are not optimized or validated biological cutoffs, and several retained candidates have very low exact-match rates. Thus inclusion does not imply uniform performance across a lineage. Reference availability, incomplete fragments and uneven family representation affect all estimates. Ordinary primers are comparators rather than independent confirmations of D performance. Concentration effects of degeneracy and sensitivity to mismatches remain unmeasured experimentally.</p><p>Stratification narrows the target and changes the non-target background; it does not demonstrate that family assays outperform suborder assays on a common panel. Three Nereidiformia family panels do not cover every family of that suborder. Archived sequences, scores and the former preprint preserve the search history without presenting all outcomes as current recommendations.</p><h2>5. Conclusion and data availability</h2><p>MiPhyllo retains 48 computational primer pairs across six target units and archives 54 other pairs. Sequences, references, matching outcomes, selection decisions, source algorithms and independent verification records are available at <a href="https://github.com/GUIBA-EX/MiPhyllo">github.com/GUIBA-EX/MiPhyllo</a>. Current identifiers follow MiPhyllo-{target}-{gene}-{S|M|L}[D]_{F|R}. Copyright © 2026 Fei Xia; repository copyright and third-party data terms apply.</p>''')
page((O/'references.html').read_text())
for i,t in enumerate(taxa,1):
 rs=[x for x in rows if x['target_taxon']==t];s=f'<h2>Appendix A{i}. {t}</h2><p>Canonical prefix: MiPhyllo-{t}-. Sequences are 5′→3′. Covered and exact counts use the full target reference denominator. Strict and loose background values are WC/GT. Ordinary primers are same-coordinate comparators.</p>'
 s+=table(['Gene/pair','bp','Covered','Exact','Strict bg','Loose bg'],[[x['gene']+'-'+x['name'].split('-')[-1],x['span'],x['covered']+'/'+x['target_species'],x['exact']+'/'+x['target_species'],x['WC']+'/'+x['GT'],x['WCloose']+'/'+x['GTloose']] for x in rs])
 s+='<h3>Primer sequences</h3>'+table(['Gene/pair','Forward','Reverse'],[[x['gene']+'-'+x['name'].split('-')[-1],x['F'],x['R']] for x in rs])
 s+='<p>Binding coordinates, accessions and per-species matches: panels/'+t+'/results.csv and species.csv. Ordinary/D counterparts share all four endpoints. No archived candidate is included in these tables.</p>';page(s)
css='''@page{size:A4;margin:16mm 16mm 17mm;@bottom-right{content:counter(page);font:8pt Arial}}body{font:10pt/1.35 Arial,sans-serif;color:#182c36}h1{font-size:22pt;line-height:1.15}h2{font-size:15pt}h3{font-size:11pt}p{margin:7pt 0}.author{text-align:center}.page{break-before:page}.page:first-child{break-before:auto}h2,h3{break-after:avoid}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:7.8pt;line-height:1.25;margin:9pt 0}th,td{padding:4pt 3pt;border-bottom:1px solid #ccd8dd;text-align:left}th{background:#e7f0f3}tr{break-inside:avoid}img{width:100%}a{color:#18566c}'''
(O/'MiPhyllo_preprint.html').write_text('<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>'+TITLE+'</title><style>'+css+'</style>'+''.join(parts))
subprocess.run(['pandoc',str(O/'MiPhyllo_preprint.html'),'-f','html','-t','gfm','-o',str(O/'MiPhyllo_preprint.md')],check=True)
(O/'MiPhyllo_manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps(dict(title=TITLE,author='Fei Xia',current_pairs=len(rows),conditional_pairs=0,archived_pairs=54,units=taxa,catalog_sha256=hashlib.sha256((R/'catalog.csv').read_bytes()).hexdigest(),selection='archive/selection_decisions.csv'),indent=2)+'\n')
print('Built curated manuscript',len(parts),'sections/pages')
